      "answer": "b",
      "explanation": "<strong>In simple words:</strong> Linear regression learns a straight-line relationship from historical examples. It uses that pattern to estimate numerical values for new cases.",
      "technicalExplanation": "<strong>Detailed explanation:</strong> Linear regression predicts a continuous numerical target using a model linear in coefficients. Simple form: y_hat=mx+b. The slope and intercept are learned from labeled history, usually by least squares. House price from area, salary from experience, and sales from advertising spend are illustrative examples. Correlation does not prove causation; predictions can fail outside the observed range."
