    {
      "tag": "Medium",
      "stem": "How does the Linear Regression equation \\(y = mx + b\\) work mathematically, what do the slope, intercept, and predicted value represent, and how are Residuals, Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) used to find the best-fitting line?",
      "figure": "<section class=\"lr-figure\" data-scene=\"lr-04-ols-error\"><span class=\"lr-kicker\">VISUAL EXPLANATION</span><h3>Slope, intercept, OLS and residuals</h3><div class=\"lr-svg-wrap\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 940 340\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Slope, intercept, OLS and residuals\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"8\" width=\"924\" height=\"324\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fcfdfe\" stroke=\"#d2e4e9\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"470\" y=\"36\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">OLS MINIMIZES SQUARED VERTICAL RESIDUALS</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"58\" width=\"557\" height=\"245\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d2e4e9\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"265\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"265\" stroke=\"#94b4c0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"265\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#94b4c0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M125 236.2 L525 92.20000000000002\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#67ad99\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"149.8\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#68b399\" stroke=\"white\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"135.4\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d9a06c\" stroke=\"white\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"365\" y1=\"149.8\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"135.4\" stroke=\"#d9a06c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"608\" y=\"58\" width=\"304\" height=\"245\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eff7fb\" stroke=\"#d2e4e9\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"760\" y=\"93\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">ŷ = mx + b</text><text x=\"760\" y=\"126\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">m=2, b=50 (in $k)</text><rect x=\"631\" y=\"158\" width=\"258\" height=\"76\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff2e5\" stroke=\"#d2e4e9\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"760\" y=\"188\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">observed 270; fitted 250</text><text x=\"760\" y=\"214\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">residual = +20</text><text x=\"761\" y=\"270\" font-family=\"Arial,system-ui\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#426b80\">OLS minimizes Σ residual²</text></svg></div><p class=\"lr-note\">Residual means actual minus fitted; <strong>OLS minimizes squared errors</strong>.</p></section>",
      "options": [
        "The slope specifies the prediction at zero input, while the intercept measures each feature unit's change.",
        "A residual is the gap between two input features, and OLS maximizes all squared errors.",
        "The slope gives change per feature unit; intercept gives baseline; OLS minimizes squared residuals.",
        "MSE ignores observed target values and is calculated using only the predicted values themselves."
      ],
      "answer": "c",
      "explanation": "<strong>In simple words:</strong> The slope gives the change per input unit, and the intercept sets the baseline. OLS finds the line with smaller squared errors.",
      "technicalExplanation": "<strong>Detailed explanation:</strong> In y_hat=mx+b, m is predicted target change per feature unit, b is the fitted value at x=0, and y_hat is the prediction. Residual e=observed−predicted; SSE=Σe² and MSE=SSE/n. OLS chooses m and b to minimize SSE. For a teaching line y_hat=2x+50 (price in thousands), x=100 predicts 250. If the actual price is 270, the residual is +20 and squared error 400."
    },
